<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Celebration · War and march · 1820 · Oceania</p><h1 class="sg-title">Ka Mate</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A haka made by the Māori chief Te Rauparaha around 1820 to celebrate staying alive, now performed by New Zealand's rugby teams.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Te Rauparaha</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Te Rauparaha</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Ngāti Toa (Māori of the North Island of New Zealand)</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Māori</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Motuopihi Island, Lake Rotoaira, North Island, New Zealand</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>around 1820</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Celebration · War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (composed by Te Rauparaha (Ngāti Toa) around 1820)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ka-mate">Ka Mate</a>&quot; is a Māori haka composed by Te Rauparaha, the leader of the iwi (tribe) of Ngāti Toa of the North Island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">Musket</a> Wars. Around 1820, pursued by enemies of Ngāti Maniapoto and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waikato">Waikato</a>, he hid on Motuopihi Island in Lake Rotoaira inside a kūmara (sweet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a>) storage pit, while a woman named Rangikoaea sat astride the pit to hide and protect him. When he climbed out into the sun he composed the haka in gratitude to Rangikoaea and her husband Te Wharerangi, as a celebration of life over death. It opens with a chant, followed by the main body of the haka. It was made as a short, energising haka of the ngeri type, which has no set movements: performers chant and move as they feel, with no need to keep together.</p><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ka-mate">Ka Mate</a>&quot; became the best-known haka in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and abroad because a choreographed, synchronised version has been performed before test matches by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-blacks">All Blacks</a>, New Zealand's rugby union team, and by the Kiwis, its rugby league team, until 2013, when the Kiwis took up &quot;Te Iwi Kiwi&quot;. Before 1985 most of the All Blacks, players of European descent, had no full training in it; two Māori players, Buck Shelford and Hika Reid, changed the way it was delivered to a style close to the original, and from then on the non-Māori players learned it. Since 2005 the All Blacks have sometimes performed another haka, &quot;Kapa o Pango&quot;, instead; their haka leader TJ Perenara explained before the 2019 Rugby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifa-world-cup">World Cup</a> that in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a> they always perform &quot;Ka Mate&quot;, in respect for Te Rauparaha and the lands he walked on. All 22 international rugby union sides that have played the All Blacks have faced it at least once.</p><p>Between 1998 and 2006 Ngāti Toa tried to register &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ka-mate">Ka Mate</a>&quot; as a trademark to stop commercial use without their permission; in 2006 the Intellectual Property Office of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> refused, on the grounds that the haka had come to represent New Zealand as a whole rather than any one trader. In 2009, as part of a wider settlement of the iwi's grievances, the New Zealand government agreed to record the authorship and significance of the haka to Ngāti Toa and to work with them on their concerns, though not to give royalties or a veto over its performance. In March 2011 the New Zealand Rugby Union reached an agreement with the iwi not to bring the mana of the haka into disrepute.</p><p>On 14 November 2024, during the first reading of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-principles-bill">Treaty Principles Bill</a>, which sought to set out interpretations of the 1840 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-waitangi">Treaty of Waitangi</a> between Māori and the Crown, the Te Pāti Māori member Hana-Rawhiti Maipi-Clarke tore a copy of the bill in half and led &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ka-mate">Ka Mate</a>&quot;, joined by her party, other opposition members and the public gallery. The Speaker, Gerry Brownlee, suspended the sitting for 20 minutes, had the gallery cleared and named her, suspending her from Parliament for 24 hours.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>The haka celebrates life over death. Composed by a chief who had just escaped his pursuers, it turns the moment of coming out of hiding into the light into a cry of survival and thanks, performed with the full energy of voice and body.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Māori</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Māori</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">Ka Mate! Ka Mate!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It is death! It is death!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">Ka Ora! Ka Ora!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It is life! It is life!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">Ka Mate! Ka Mate!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It is death! It is death!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">Ka Ora! Ka Ora!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It is life! It is life!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">Tenei te tangata</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>This is the man</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">Puhuruhuru nana nei i tiki mai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The hairy one, who himself fetched</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">Whakawhiti te ra!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And made the sun shine!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">Upane, ka Upane</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>One step up, another step up</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">Upane, ka Upane</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>One step up, another step up</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">Whiti te ra!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The sun shines!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Te Rauparaha, Ngāti Toa Rangatira (c. 1820); the text as set out in the Haka Ka Mate Attribution Act 2014 (New Zealand). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Haka_Ka_Mate_Attribution_Act_2014" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (5)</summary><ul><li>line 1.1-4: 'Ka mate' / 'Ka ora' can be read as 'I die' / 'I live' or 'it is death' / 'it is life'; Te Rauparaha is said to have chanted it while hiding from his pursuers, unsure whether he would be found.</li><li>line 1.6: the source splits the phrase 'tangata puhuruhuru / nana nei i tiki mai / whakawhiti te ra' differently from the usual lineation; translated line by line as given. 'Puhuruhuru' is hairy; who the hairy man is (traditionally the chief Te Wharerangi, who sheltered him, though this is debated) is disputed, so it stays literal.</li><li>line 1.7: 'Whakawhiti te ra' is 'caused the sun to shine (or to cross over)'; the clause is 'who fetched (the sun) and made it shine'.</li><li>line 1.8-9: 'Upane' is a step, a terrace or a rank (of warriors); 'step by step, upward' is the usual sense, but the word is disputed.</li><li>Macrons: the Act's text has none (Tenei for Tēnei, Puhuruhuru for Pūhuruhuru, Nana for Nāna, ra for rā); left as it stands.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
